          "type": "turn",
          "question": "A note says hypertension and chronic kidney disease. Does the classification assume a relationship between them, and what changes if it does?",
          "answer": "**Yes.** Under the classification, a record that lists hypertension with chronic kidney disease (CKD) is coded as a combination condition. The coding guidance says that once organ involvement is documented, the classification prefers a combination code rather than the plain hypertension code (coding-hypertension-i10). Hypertension with CKD moves to **I12**, with a second code for the stage of kidney disease.\n\n**What changes:**\n- **Code choice:** Hypertension alone uses I10. Hypertension with CKD uses I12, not I10.\n- **Second code:** I12 needs a separate code for the CKD stage, taken from the kidney chapter, where CKD is subdivided by stage (ICD-10-CM Chapter 14, N17 to N19). The retrieved passages do not give the specific stage code, so I can't name it here.\n- **Other combinations:** If heart disease attributed to hypertension is also documented, the case moves to I13 (both together). If hypertension is attributed to a specific underlying cause, such as a kidney cause, it is coded as secondary hypertension (I15) together with that cause (coding-hypertension-i10).\n\n**About the assumed relationship:** A coding guide for type 2 diabetes describes a general \"with\" convention. When the index links two conditions with \"with,\" the classification reads this as \"associated with\" or \"due to,\" and presumes the link unless the record says the conditions are unrelated (coding-type-2-diabetes-e11). That passage is about diabetes, and it calls the convention general. The passages I retrieved don't quote the hypertension index entry directly, so I can't confirm the exact wording for hypertension. The I12 rule itself is clear in the hypertension guidance, though.\n\n**Limits:** These passages explain how records are coded, not what a clinician has concluded about this person's health. Whether the two conditions are linked in this case would be a question for the treating clinician who wrote the note.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
